{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffJbrAQTzMymdJ22Cdkb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote and checked a new candidate model, **`run_length_profile_typicality`**. It passes the self-check, but it probably fits worse than the current best model: its ELPD-LOO on the short test fit was −2861.9, against −2369.5 for the best. The test fit is too short to settle that.\n\n**The idea:** a sequence breaks into streaks of identical flips (runs). Each person expects a fair coin to produce a typical mix of run lengths: mostly single flips, fewer pairs, rarer triples, and only occasionally a streak of four or more. A sequence looks random to the extent that its share of runs of length 1, 2, 3 and 4+ matches that expected mix. This catches three kinds of non-random sequence with one mechanism:\n- strict alternation, which is all single runs;\n- blocks like HHTTHHTT, which are all pairs;\n- long streaks.\n\nNone of the models in the current set looks at run-length mixes. Their ideas are switch rate, how far the running heads-minus-tails count swings, H/T balance, rigged-coin Bayes, and chunk variety.\n\n**How it relates to the critique of the best model:**\n- People prefer the sequence that switches more, even when the best model's measure scores the two sequences about the same. Run-length mix tells such pairs apart.\n- People differ in how much they avoid streaks. This model lets each person have their own expected mix and their own sensitivity to a mismatch.\n- It does not address the third finding, that streak avoidance changes over the session. A model that used trial position would be rejected at admission.\n\n**What it's built from:** each person has two parameters with population-level distributions. One is their felt chance that a run continues, which sets their expected mix. The other is how strongly a mismatch drives their choice. Each sequence is scored by how far its run-length shares are from that expected mix, and the person picks the sequence that is closer.\n\n**Check result:** the self-check printed OK. The model loads and has a finite log-probability on the real responses. It meets the data contract, and the short fit (100 draws, 1 chain) gives a finite ELPD-LOO. Two warnings:\n- 7.4% of trials have high Pareto-k values, above the 1% tolerance, so the ELPD-LOO estimate isn't reliable yet.\n- Convergence couldn't be verified with one chain.\n\nAdmission's full fit will decide both. The check doesn't test whether the model's predictions differ enough from the existing models' to count as new.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2744,"cache_read_input_tokens":64448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2744,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbf12c1d-0e82-4b10-a5d7-20503aa1eaed","uuid":"186b79fb-f87b-4c87-989e-47ae590a0a20","timestamp":"2026-10-03T12:28:58.818Z","request_id":"req_011CffJbqjcNNDpFCGaGd6QG"}
